    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The given ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\), meaning the center of the ellipse is at \\((4, 1)\\) and the semi-major axis is 4. Similarly, the ellipse is tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\), meaning the center is also at \\((4, 1)\\) and the semi-minor axis is 1.\n\nThe standard form of the equation of an ellipse with its center at \\((h, k)\\) and semi-major and semi-minor axes \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) respectively, is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nHere, the center \\((h, k)\\) is \\((4, 1)\\), and the semi-major and semi-minor axes are 4 and 1, respectively. Thus, the equation of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{16} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Here, \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = 1\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{4^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
