    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given the ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\), we can infer the properties of the ellipse. The standard form of an ellipse with axes parallel to the coordinate axes is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the semi-major axis and \\(b\\) is the semi-minor axis. The ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\), meaning the distance from the center to the \\(x\\)-axis is \\(a = 4\\). The ellipse is tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\), meaning the distance from the center to the \\(y\\)-axis is \\(b = 1\\).\n\nThus, the coordinates of the center of the ellipse are \\((2, 0.5)\\). Substituting \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = 1\\) into the standard form of the ellipse equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{4^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
